course: dictionary
lesson: 10835
title: Verbs: doing and being words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A verb is a word that tells us what someone or something does. In «The dog barked», the verb is barked, because it tells us what the dog did. Verbs like run, eat and think are doing words.
explain: Some verbs tell us what something is or has, not what it does. In «She is happy» the verb is is, and in «They have a dog» the verb is have. Teachers call these being words, and they are still verbs.
explain: Every full sentence needs a verb. Verbs change for time, so «walk», «walked» and «will walk» show now, before and later. To find the verb, change the time of the sentence and see which word changes.
fact: A verb is a doing word or a being word.
fact: «Run», «eat» and «think» are doing verbs.
fact: «Is», «seem» and «have» can show what something is or has.
fact: Every full sentence needs a verb; in «The dog barked», the verb is barked.
fact: Helping verbs such as «is», «have» and «will» work with other verbs, and irregular verbs change in unusual ways, like «go» becoming «went».
exercise: «The dog barked.» Which word is the verb? | *barked | The | dog
exercise: «She is happy.» Which word is a being verb? | *is | happy | She
exercise: «Yesterday we walked to school.» Which word shows the past? | *walked | school | We
test: In «The children ran home», what is the verb? | *ran | children | home
test: «They will play outside.» Which word is a helping verb? | play | outside | They | *will
exam: Which sentence uses a being verb, not a doing verb? | The dog runs fast. | *The sky is blue. | Birds sing loudly.
